{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Uterine sarcoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the muscles of the uterus or other tissues that support the uterus.","source_span":"Uterine sarcoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the muscles of the uterus or other tissues that support the uterus."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Being exposed to x-rays can increase the risk of uterine sarcoma.","source_span":"Being exposed to x-rays can increase the risk of uterine sarcoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Past treatment with radiation therapy to the pelvis is a risk factor for uterine sarcoma.","source_span":"Past treatment with radiation therapy to the pelvis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Treatment with tamoxifen for breast cancer is a risk factor for uterine sarcoma.","source_span":"Treatment with tamoxifen for breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Women taking tamoxifen should have a pelvic exam every year and report any vaginal bleeding other than menstrual bleeding as soon as possible.","source_span":"have a pelvic exam every year and report any vaginal bleeding (other than menstrual bleeding) as soon as possible."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Signs of uterine sarcoma include bleeding not part of menstrual periods, bleeding after menopause, a mass in the vagina, pain or fullness in the abdomen, and frequent urination.","source_span":"Bleeding that is not part of menstrual periods.    - Bleeding after menopause.    - A mass in the vagina.    - Pain or a feeling of fullness in the abdomen.    - Frequent urination."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"The CA 125 assay is a test used in staging that measures the level of CA 125 in the blood.","source_span":"CA 125 assay : A test that measures the level of CA 125 in the blood."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Uterine sarcoma may be diagnosed, staged, and treated in the same surgery.","source_span":"Uterine sarcoma may be diagnosed, staged, and treated in the same surgery."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"There are four stages of uterine sarcoma: Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV.","source_span":"The following stages are used for uterine sarcoma:         - Stage I     - Stage II     - Stage III     - Stage IV"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for uterine sarcoma depend on the stage of the cancer, the type and size of the tumor, the patient's general health, and whether the cancer is newly diagnosed or recurred.","source_span":"The stage of the cancer.    - The type and size of the tumor.    - The patient's general health.    - Whether the cancer has just been diagnosed or has recurred (come back)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Dilatation and curettage (D&C) is a procedure to remove samples of tissue from the inner lining of the uterus, using a curette after the cervix is dilated.","source_span":"Dilatation and curettage : A procedure to remove samples of tissue from the inner lining of the uterus. The cervix is dilated and a curette (spoon-shaped instrument) is inserted into the uterus to remove tissue."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","statement":"Because uterine sarcoma begins inside the uterus, it may not show up on a Pap test.","source_span":"Because uterine sarcoma begins inside the uterus, this cancer may not show up on the Pap test."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[1,2,3],"question":"What factors increase a person's risk of developing uterine sarcoma?","answer":"Risk factors for uterine sarcoma include being exposed to x-rays, past treatment with radiation therapy to the pelvis, and treatment with tamoxifen for breast cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What should a woman taking tamoxifen for breast cancer do to monitor for uterine sarcoma?","answer":"Because tamoxifen treatment is a risk factor for uterine sarcoma, women taking it should have a pelvic exam every year and report any vaginal bleeding other than menstrual bleeding as soon as possible."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the signs and symptoms of uterine sarcoma?","answer":"Signs include bleeding that is not part of menstrual periods, bleeding after menopause, a mass in the vagina, pain or a feeling of fullness in the abdomen, and frequent urination."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What does a dilatation and curettage (D&C) procedure involve?","answer":"A D&C removes samples of tissue from the inner lining of the uterus: the cervix is dilated and a spoon-shaped instrument called a curette is inserted into the uterus to remove tissue, which is then checked under a microscope."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are the four stages used to classify uterine sarcoma?","answer":"Uterine sarcoma is staged as Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, or Stage IV."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What factors affect the prognosis and treatment options for uterine sarcoma?","answer":"Prognosis and treatment depend on the stage of the cancer, the type and size of the tumor, the patient's general health, and whether the cancer is newly diagnosed or has recurred."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_2","fact_refs":[11],"question":"Why might a Pap test fail to detect uterine sarcoma?","answer":"Because uterine sarcoma begins inside the uterus rather than on the surface of the cervix or vagina, it may not show up on a Pap test."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Endometrial cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the endometrium.","source_span":"Endometrial cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the endometrium."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Endometrial cancer is the most common invasive cancer of the female reproductive system.","source_span":"Endometrial cancer is the most common invasive cancer of the female reproductive system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Endometrial cancer is diagnosed most often in postmenopausal women at an average age of 60 years.","source_span":"Endometrial cancer is diagnosed most often in postmenopausal women at an average age of 60 years"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"From 2004 to 2013, the number of new cases of endometrial cancer increased slightly in white and African American women.","source_span":"From 2004 to 2013, the number of new cases of endometrial cancer increased slightly in white and African American women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"From 2005 to 2014, the number of deaths from endometrial cancer also increased slightly in white and African American women.","source_span":"From 2005 to 2014, the number of deaths from endometrial cancer also increased slightly in white and African American women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Compared with white women, rates of endometrial cancer are lower in Japanese Americans and Latinas.","source_span":"Compared with white women, rates of endometrial cancer are lower in Japanese Americans and in Latinas."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"The number of deaths from endometrial cancer is higher in African American women compared with women of other races.","source_span":"The number of deaths from endometrial cancer is higher in African American women compared with women of other races."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Using tamoxifen for more than 2 years increases the risk of endometrial cancer, and this risk is greater in postmenopausal women.","source_span":"using tamoxifen for more than 2 years increases the risk of endometrial cancer. This risk is greater in postmenopausal women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Raloxifene does not have estrogen-like effects on the uterus and has not been shown to increase the risk of endometrial cancer.","source_span":"it does not have estrogen-like effects on the uterus and has not been shown to increase the risk of endometrial cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"For postmenopausal women, taking estrogen in combination with progestin does not increase the risk of endometrial cancer, but does increase the risk of breast cancer.","source_span":"taking estrogen in combination with progestin does not increase the risk of endometrial cancer, but it does increase the risk of breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Estrogen-only hormone replacement therapy (HRT) increases the risk of endometrial cancer and endometrial hyperplasia, and also increases the risk of stroke and blood clots.","source_span":"The use of HRT that contains only estrogen increases the risk of endometrial cancer and endometrial hyperplasia... HRT that contains only estrogen also increases the risk of stroke and blood clots."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Protective factors that decrease the risk of endometrial cancer include pregnancy and breast-feeding, combination oral contraceptives, physical activity, and cigarette smoking.","source_span":"The following protective factors decrease the risk of endometrial cancer:         - Pregnancy and breast-feeding     - Combination oral contraceptives     - Physical activity     - Cigarette smoking"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","statement":"Hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (HNPCC) syndrome, also known as Lynch Syndrome, is an inherited disorder that gives women who have it a much higher risk of developing endometrial cancer.","source_span":"Hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (HNPCC) syndrome (also known as Lynch Syndrome) is an inherited disorder caused by changes in certain genes. Women who have HNPCC syndrome have a much higher risk of developing endometri"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[2],"question":"At what average age is endometrial cancer typically diagnosed?","answer":"Endometrial cancer is diagnosed most often in postmenopausal women at an average age of 60 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How do endometrial cancer rates in Japanese American and Latina women compare with rates in white women?","answer":"Compared with white women, rates of endometrial cancer are lower in Japanese Americans and in Latinas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How does using tamoxifen for more than 2 years affect endometrial cancer risk?","answer":"Using tamoxifen for more than 2 years increases the risk of endometrial cancer, and this increased risk is greater in postmenopausal women."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Does combination estrogen-progestin hormone therapy increase a postmenopausal woman's risk of endometrial cancer?","answer":"No — for postmenopausal women, taking estrogen combined with progestin does not increase the risk of endometrial cancer, though it does increase the risk of breast cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What factors are known to decrease (protect against) the risk of endometrial cancer?","answer":"Protective factors include pregnancy and breast-feeding, combination oral contraceptives, physical activity, and cigarette smoking."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[12],"question":"What inherited genetic condition increases the risk of endometrial cancer, and what is its other name?","answer":"Hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (HNPCC) syndrome, also known as Lynch Syndrome, is an inherited disorder that gives women who have it a much higher risk of developing endometrial cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000014_3","fact_refs":[4,6],"question":"How did endometrial cancer deaths change among African American women from 2005 to 2014, and how do death rates compare to other races?","answer":"From 2005 to 2014, deaths from endometrial cancer increased slightly among African American women, and the overall number of deaths from endometrial cancer is higher in African American women than in women of other races."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Childhood extracranial germ cell tumors form from germ cells in parts of the body other than the brain.","source_span":"Childhood extracranial germ cell tumors form from germ cells in parts of the body other than the brain."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Extracranial germ cell tumors usually form in the testicles, ovaries, sacrum or coccyx, retroperitoneum, or mediastinum.","source_span":"Extracranial germ cell tumors usually form in the following areas of the body:          - Testicles.    - Ovaries.    -  Sacrum or coccyx (bottom part of the spine).    - Retroperitoneum (the back wall of the abdomen).    -  Mediastinum (area between the lungs)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Extracranial germ cell tumors are most common in adolescents 15 to 19 years of age.","source_span":"Extracranial germ cell tumors are most common in adolescents 15 to 19 years of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"There are three types of extracranial germ cell tumors: mature teratomas, immature teratomas, and malignant germ cell tumors.","source_span":"Extracranial germ cell tumors are grouped into mature teratomas, immature teratomas, and malignant germ cell tumors"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Mature teratomas are the most common type of extracranial germ cell tumor and are benign, not likely to become cancer.","source_span":"Mature teratomas are the most common type of extracranial germ cell tumor. Mature teratomas are benign tumors and not likely to become cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Germinomas are tumors that make a hormone called beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG).","source_span":"Germinomas: Tumors that make a hormone called beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (-hCG)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Of the three types of germinomas, dysgerminomas form in the ovary in girls, seminomas form in the testicle in boys, and germinomas form in areas of the body that are not the ovary or testicle.","source_span":"Dysgerminomas form in the ovary in girls.       -  Seminomas form in the testicle in boys.       - Germinomas form in areas of the body that are not the ovary or testicle."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Yolk sac tumors make a hormone called alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and can form in the ovary, testicle, or other areas of the body.","source_span":"Yolk sac tumors make a hormone called alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). They can form in the ovary, testicle, or other areas of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Choriocarcinomas make a hormone called beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG) and can form in the ovary, testicle, or other areas of the body.","source_span":"Choriocarcinomas make a hormone called beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (-hCG). They can form in the ovary, testicle, or other areas of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Testicular germ cell tumors usually occur before the age of 4 years or in adolescents and young adults.","source_span":"Testicular germ cell tumors usually occur before the age of 4 years or in adolescents and young adults."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Boys older than 14 years with testicular germ cell tumors are treated in pediatric cancer centers, but the treatment is much like the treatment used in adults.","source_span":"Boys older than 14 years with testicular germ cell tumors are treated in pediatric cancer centers, but the treatment is much like the treatment used in adults."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Most ovarian germ cell tumors are benign teratomas.","source_span":"Most ovarian germ cell tumors are benign teratomas."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Testicular germ cell tumors are divided into two main types: seminoma and nonseminoma.","source_span":"Testicular germ cell tumors are divided into two main types, seminoma and nonseminoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","statement":"Nonseminomas are usually large and cause signs or symptoms, and they tend to grow and spread more quickly than seminomas.","source_span":"Nonseminomas are usually large and cause signs or symptoms. They tend to grow and spread more quickly than seminomas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In which age group are extracranial germ cell tumors most common?","answer":"Extracranial germ cell tumors are most common in adolescents 15 to 19 years of age."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the three types of childhood extracranial germ cell tumors?","answer":"The three types are mature teratomas, immature teratomas, and malignant germ cell tumors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What hormone do yolk sac tumors produce, and where can they form?","answer":"Yolk sac tumors make a hormone called alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), and they can form in the ovary, testicle, or other areas of the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Where do dysgerminomas, seminomas, and germinomas each typically form?","answer":"Dysgerminomas form in the ovary in girls, seminomas form in the testicle in boys, and germinomas form in areas of the body that are not the ovary or testicle."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"At what ages do testicular germ cell tumors usually occur?","answer":"Testicular germ cell tumors usually occur before the age of 4 years or in adolescents and young adults."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[10],"question":"How are boys older than 14 years with testicular germ cell tumors treated?","answer":"They are treated in pediatric cancer centers, but the treatment is much like the treatment used in adults."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000015_1","fact_refs":[12,13],"question":"What is the difference between seminomas and nonseminomas among testicular germ cell tumors?","answer":"Testicular germ cell tumors are divided into seminoma and nonseminoma; nonseminomas are usually large and cause signs or symptoms, and they tend to grow and spread more quickly than seminomas."}
